(2834776, 391113, 714, '<td valign="top">hahahah..she is so cute! I love those faces children make.</td>', 'hahahah..she is so cute! I love those faces children make.', '2006-09-14 13:15:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(2834949, 390886, 40718, '<td valign="top">OK, let''s take a look.  I gave it a 7, so apparently I liked it more than the average voter.<br/> <br/>The technicals:<br/>Composition is well thought out.  Slightly off centered main subject and nicely split background.  Lighting is most excellent (one of the things I appreciated).  Just the right amount of highlighting.  Focus is also excellent.  Use of DOF is just fine.  A shallow DOF would not have helped this picture.  Colors are nicely done as well.  Definitely would not have been improved in B&amp;W.<br/> <br/>The feel:<br/>Qualifies as a simple pleasure in my book.  Something small and delightful.  Your border is nice, although it''s getting "up there" in complexity and I just find that more complex is bad on DPC.  I like the matched pixel stroke, but not everybody does.  The title, I think, was a poor choice.  It may have hurt you in it''s ambiguity and "look" (all those dots and the question mark just aren''t "artistic).<br/> <br/>The game:<br/>Well, since you have the technicals down just fine and I think you easily met the challenge and have a good feel (except for the title), it''s the game you lost out on.  The picture does lack "wow".  Now "wow" doesn''t have to be some crazy cool landscape we''ve never seen, but a great picture will have "impact".  I guess you can look at the top three, including mine.  They all had emotional resonance, which this picture lacks.  Someone else noted on a thread that sepia was great for this challenge because of the implied emotional feeling.  Maybe that''s what people were looking for in this challenge.  Your picture was very well done and personally I think you got robbed a bit on the score (I don''t give out even 7s lightly (I gave out 27 in this challenge with 3 8s and 1 9).  If I could assign a score to this shot, I''d give it a 6.4.  A great score, but the lack of impact along with the poor title limit it from being a 6.5+.</td>', 'OK, let''s take a look.  I gave it a 7, so apparently I liked it more than the average voter. The technicals:Composition is well thought out.  Slightly off centered main subject and nicely split background.  Lighting is most excellent (one of the things I appreciated).  Just the right amount of highlighting.  Focus is also excellent.  Use of DOF is just fine.  A shallow DOF would not have helped this picture.  Colors are nicely done as well.  Definitely would not have been improved in B&W. The feel:Qualifies as a simple pleasure in my book.  Something small and delightful.  Your border is nice, although it''s getting "up there" in complexity and I just find that more complex is bad on DPC.  I like the matched pixel stroke, but not everybody does.  The title, I think, was a poor choice.  It may have hurt you in it''s ambiguity and "look" (all those dots and the question mark just aren''t "artistic). The game:Well, since you have the technicals down just fine and I think you easily met the challenge and have a good feel (except for the title), it''s the game you lost out on.  The picture does lack "wow".  Now "wow" doesn''t have to be some crazy cool landscape we''ve never seen, but a great picture will have "impact".  I guess you can look at the top three, including mine.  They all had emotional resonance, which this picture lacks.  Someone else noted on a thread that sepia was great for this challenge because of the implied emotional feeling.  Maybe that''s what people were looking for in this challenge.  Your picture was very well done and personally I think you got robbed a bit on the score (I don''t give out even 7s lightly (I gave out 27 in this challenge with 3 8s and 1 9).  If I could assign a score to this shot, I''d give it a 6.4.  A great score, but the lack of impact along with the poor title limit it from being a 6.5+.', '2006-09-14 14:44:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(2834953, 391190, 1031, '<td valign="top">I considered submitting some whipped cream and a cherry and your title -- now I''m glad I chose something else, since mine probably would''ve finished way lower. Congratulations on a nice simple and effective shot.</td>', 'I considered submitting some whipped cream and a cherry and your title -- now I''m glad I chose something else, since mine probably would''ve finished way lower. Congratulations on a nice simple and effective shot.', '2006-09-14 14:46:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(2834974, 390239, 16046, '<td valign="top">Excellent B/W shows effective lighting. Congrats!</td>', 'Excellent B/W shows effective lighting. Congrats!', '2006-09-14 14:55:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(2834988, 390501, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/> <br/>Exposure, lighting and contrast are all good in this piece.  Focus and depth of field are done very well.<br/> <br/>I wouldn''t have amputated the diver''s feet.  He''s going to need them.  The person in the water is a distraction where (s)he is.  Either a different camera angle, or relocating the swimmer so that it looks like the diver is taking the plunge to join the swimmer, would have worked well.  I would not remove the swimmer.  For me, the swimmer helps tell the story as to why the diver is taking the plunge.  However, at this angle and distance, the swimmer tends to pull the eye out of the frame.<br/> <br/>Although this was a basic edititng challenge, were I going for an advanced challenge, or going to print the image for my wall, I would have removed the small island in the background.  It breaks up the smooth flow of the shorline a bit, and without it the solitude is magnified.  <br/> <br/>Overall I like the photo a lot.  It tells a story, and captures the feeling of the title.  Good job!</td>', 'Critique Club Review: Exposure, lighting and contrast are all good in this piece.  Focus and depth of field are done very well. I wouldn''t have amputated the diver''s feet.  He''s going to need them.  The person in the water is a distraction where (s)he is.  Either a different camera angle, or relocating the swimmer so that it looks like the diver is taking the plunge to join the swimmer, would have worked well.  I would not remove the swimmer.  For me, the swimmer helps tell the story as to why the diver is taking the plunge.  However, at this angle and distance, the swimmer tends to pull the eye out of the frame. Although this was a basic edititng challenge, were I going for an advanced challenge, or going to print the image for my wall, I would have removed the small island in the background.  It breaks up the smooth flow of the shorline a bit, and without it the solitude is magnified.   Overall I like the photo a lot.  It tells a story, and captures the feeling of the title.  Good job!', '2006-09-14 15:01:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(2834989, 388063, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by irakly:</b></div><hr/><i>For the members of the Critique Club: before you say something, please realize that some people know a thing or two about photography. It will help you avoid ridiculing yourself and looking as ignorant as the bloke who left his priceless thoughts under my last photo.</i></td></tr></table> <br/>This statement seems unnecessarily confrontational -- I find likely to divert the CC member''s attention away from your interesting photo, and to a consideration of your attitude, just as it has mine.<br/> <br/>FWIW I "got" the context of a class/club and their model taking a break without much trouble ... the processing gives it a look which reminds me of an etching or other "old-timey" print, which I think works well for this image.</td>', 'Originally posted by irakly:For the members of the Critique Club: before you say something, please realize that some people know a thing or two about photography. It will help you avoid ridiculing yourself and looking as ignorant as the bloke who left his priceless thoughts under my last photo. This statement seems unnecessarily confrontational -- I find likely to divert the CC member''s attention away from your interesting photo, and to a consideration of your attitude, just as it has mine. FWIW I "got" the context of a class/club and their model taking a break without much trouble ... the processing gives it a look which reminds me of an etching or other "old-timey" print, which I think works well for this image.', '2006-09-14 15:01:24.000000', NULL, 0),
